**Android can't tell you who touched your phone. It keeps no list of who tried to unlock it, and it never takes a photo.** To find out, you need an app that uses the front camera at the lock screen. CrookCatcher takes a photo when someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password, and emails it to you with the location, so you see it even when the phone isn't with you. It has to be installed before it happens, so the next attempt is the one you catch.

## What Android shows on its own

Not much. When someone enters a wrong PIN, Android rejects it and, after several tries, makes the person wait. It doesn't save the attempts anywhere you can look, and it doesn't use the camera. Here's [exactly what Android does with failed unlock attempts](/blog/failed-unlock-attempts-android).

If someone got in, there are traces: Digital Wellbeing counts how often the phone was unlocked and which apps were opened, and your browser and Google activity show what was done. See [how to tell if someone went through your Android phone](/blog/signs-someone-went-through-your-phone). None of it shows who it was.

## How to get a photo of whoever touched your phone

Someone who picks up a phone that isn't theirs usually tries a PIN or two. CrookCatcher reacts at that moment. Each time an unlock attempt fails, it:

- takes a photo with the front camera of whoever is holding the phone;
- records the GPS location and an approximate address;
- emails the photo and location to you. If the phone is online, the email goes out right away; if not, as soon as it reconnects.

