## If you think someone knows your PIN

A photo app catches people who have to guess. Someone who already knows your PIN doesn't have to. So:

- **Change your PIN, pattern or password.** On a Pixel it's under `Settings > Security & privacy > Device unlock > Screen lock`; on other phones, search Settings for "Screen lock". Pick one that isn't your birthday or an easy pattern.
- **Check your fingerprints and faces.** In the same settings, make sure only your own fingerprints and face are set up.
- **Keep CrookCatcher running.** Once the old PIN stops working, anyone who tries it is photographed. With break-in detection, you also get a photo if they then guess the new one.

## Other apps with this name

The best-known app called "Who Touched My Phone" is WTMP, by Mindfulness Devs. Its Google Play listing describes a report saved in the app each time the phone is unlocked or someone tries to, with a front camera photo and the apps that were opened. You read the reports on the phone. For how it compares with CrookCatcher and other apps, see [the best intruder selfie apps for Android](/blog/best-intruder-selfie-apps-android).

## Test it

Whatever app you use, test it: lock your phone, enter a wrong PIN on purpose, unlock it and check that your own photo is there. Test again a week later, because some phones stop background apps after a few days.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### Q: Can Android show who touched my phone?
A: No. Android has no log of who tried to unlock the phone and never takes a photo. Digital Wellbeing shows how many times the phone was unlocked, but not by whom.

### Q: How do I know if someone touched my phone while it was out of my sight?
A: Check Digital Wellbeing afterwards for unlocks you didn't make. For proof of who it was, install CrookCatcher beforehand, so the next wrong PIN is photographed.

### Q: Is there a free app that shows who touched my phone?
A: Yes. CrookCatcher's photo, location and email alert are free. Pro adds break-in detection, video with sound and Google Drive backup.

### Q: Does it work with fingerprint or face unlock?
A: Yes, you can keep using both. CrookCatcher reacts to a wrong PIN, pattern or password; a failed fingerprint or face attempt doesn't trigger a photo.

### Q: Can I see who touched my phone if they knew my PIN?
A: Not with a photo, unless they got it wrong first: then CrookCatcher Pro's break-in detection photographs them when they get in. Change your PIN, so anyone who tries the old one is caught.

### Q: Why didn't the app take a photo?
A: Most often the phone's battery saver stopped the app in the background, or the phone was just restarted. After a restart, Android only lets apps use the camera once the phone has been unlocked one time.

